{"name":"Spice Route","hex":"#c26e4b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Aromatic or pungent plant matter (usually dried) used to season or flavor food.","The quality of being spicy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add spice or spices to; season.","To spice up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spice"},{"term":"route","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A course or way which is traveled or passed.","A regular itinerary of stops, or the path followed between these stops, such as for delivery or passenger transportation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To direct or divert along a particular course.","to connect two local area networks, thereby forming an internet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/route"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spice trade","blocks":[{"text":"The spice trade involved historical civilizations in Asia, Northeast Africa and Europe. Spices, such as cinnamon, cassia, cardamom, ginger, pepper, nutmeg, star anise, clove, and turmeric, were known and used in antiquity and traded in the Eastern world. These spices found their way into the Near East before the beginning of the Christian era, with fantastic tales hiding their true sources."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Silk_route.jpg/330px-Silk_route.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice_trade","wikidata":"Q1778604"},{"title":"Spice","blocks":[{"text":"In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavouring or colouring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavouring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. \nSpices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing. Plant-based sweeteners such as sugar are not considered spices."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Spices1.jpg/330px-Spices1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice","wikidata":"Q42527"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg/500px-Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg/500px-Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The spice trade from India attracted the attention of the Ptolemaic Kingdom, and subsequently the Roman Empire.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png/500px-Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png/1280px-Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png/500px-Austronesian_maritime_trade_network_in_the_Indian_Ocean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Austronesian proto-historic and historic maritime trade network in the Indian Ocean[10]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustronesian_maritime_trade_netwo